          <h3>The Congo African Greys We Have Available Today</h3>
          <p>Congos are the larger of the two, with the scarlet tail most people picture when they hear the name, and we have {groupCount("congo") + groupCount("pair")} of them including the pair. Bery is grown and settled. Amie is still on formula and finishes weaning in our kitchen. Roys is the busy one, the bird who will dismantle a foraging toy while the others watch.</p>

          <h4>What Does Each Congo African Grey Cost?</h4>
          <p>Congos here run from {congoRange.split("–")[0]}, and the bonded pair is {congoRange.split("–")[1]} because two birds leave together. The spread inside that range is about age and about which bird has already done the growing, not about a sliding scale we apply to whoever asks. The number on the card is the number you pay.</p>

          <h5>Every Congo Price Includes Sexing, Paperwork, and a Vet Certificate</h5>
          <p>Nothing on the list in the receipt above is an extra. A bird that has been examined, screened, sexed and papered costs us more to raise than a bird that has only been fed, and pricing those steps separately would let us advertise a smaller headline while charging the same money in the end. We would rather print one figure and stand behind it.</p>

          <h6>Price Check: What a Congo Price Should Always Include</h6>
          <p>Ask any seller to itemise, and listen for four things: hand-feeding through to weaning, an avian examination before sale, a sexing result on paper and the treaty documentation. If the reply covers only the bird, the rest was either skipped or is coming as a surprise invoice later. <a href="/african-grey-parrot-price/">Our costs page</a> sets the same arithmetic out across a first year rather than a single purchase.</p>

          <h3>The Timneh African Greys We Have Available Today</h3>
          <p>Timnehs are the smaller subspecies, darker in the body, with a maroon tail and a horn-coloured upper beak instead of the Congo's solid black. Elad and Evie are both here, both weaned, and both priced in the {timnehRange} band.</p>

          <h4>Is a Timneh a Cheaper African Grey or a Different Bird?</h4>
          <p>A different bird that happens to cost less. The price gap follows demand and size rather than quality, and the birds themselves differ in temperament: ours tend to start talking earlier and take a room's changes more calmly. Buying one to save money is the wrong reason. Buying one because a steadier, slightly smaller parrot suits your household is the right one.</p>

          <h5>Timneh Pricing Reflects Size and Demand, Not a Lesser Bird</h5>
          <p>The same feeding, the same examination and the same paperwork go into a Timneh as into a Congo, so the margin on one is thinner rather than fatter. What moves the price is that fewer buyers arrive asking for them by name, which is a fact about the market and not about the parrot sitting on the perch.</p>

          <h6>Price Check: Why Our Timnehs Start at {timnehRange.split("–")[0]}</h6>
          <p>That figure is the floor for this whole aviary, and it exists because below it the arithmetic of raising a bird properly stops working. When you see a Timneh advertised at half of it, the saving is coming out of something — the feeding, the testing, the paperwork, or the truth of the listing itself.</p>

          <h3>Jins and Jeni — the Bonded Pair We Sell Together</h3>
          <p>Jins and Jeni are not siblings and were not paired by us; they chose each other in the flight cage and have been inseparable since. They go as a pair at {congoRange.split("–")[1]} for both. We have had buyers ask to split them, and the answer is no: separating a bonded pair undoes years of the thing that makes them worth having.</p>

          <h4>What Does “Hand-Raised” Mean Here?</h4>
          <p>We call our own way of doing it <em>The Benjamin Home-Raising Protocol</em>: chicks raised in the house rather than a barn, hand-fed on a schedule we keep ourselves, and held back until they are weaned and eating unaided somewhere between twelve and sixteen weeks. It is our name for our practice, not a certificate anybody issued us, and the reason we name it at all is that “hand-raised” has become a word sellers use without doing any of it.</p>

          <h3>What Every Bird Card Tells You Before You Ask</h3>
          <p>Each card above carries the same six facts, in the same places, so you can compare birds instead of decoding listings. Name, subspecies, sex, price, what travels with the bird, and the delivery tiers. If a detail you need is missing, it is missing from our records too, and we will say so rather than fill the gap with something plausible.</p>
