        <!-- ===================== §2 · THE PRICE ARTIFACT (MANDATORY) ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="price">
          <h2>Why the Same Bird Carries Four Different Price Tags</h2>
          <p class="opener">On one afternoon in August 2026 we recorded what this search was actually asking for an African Grey, and the answers ranged from eight hundred and fifty dollars to eight and a half thousand. The wide part is not a market finding its level. Some of it is the same seller, on the same day, quoting three different numbers on three different platforms.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "buy-african-grey-price-ladder-850-to-8500-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "buy-african-grey-price-ladder-850-to-8500-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "buy-african-grey-price-ladder-850-to-8500-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Price ladder showing African Grey listings from $850 to $8,500 with the C.A.Gs band marked">

          <div class="tblwrap">
            <table class="tblH">
              <caption class="tblH-cap">The price ladder, as recorded on a single day in August 2026</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Where the price came from</th><th scope="col">Asking price</th><th scope="col">Recorded</th><th scope="col">What the price was said to include</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {ladderRows.map((r) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td data-label="Source">{r.src}</td>
                    <td data-label="Price">{r.price}</td>
                    <td data-label="Recorded">{r.when}</td>
                    <td data-label="Includes">{r.incl}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="cap">Third-party figures are readings from one afternoon and will have moved since. Ours are current and sit in the data file this page reads from.</p>

          <h3>One Seller, Three Platforms, Three Different Prices</h3>
          <p>The clearest thing we found was not the cheap end. It was one aviary advertising its birds at four and a half thousand on a classified site, seven and a half on its own website and eight and a half through an aggregator, all live at once. No bird changed. Only the audience did.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "one-seller-three-platform-prices-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "one-seller-three-platform-prices-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "one-seller-three-platform-prices-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="One seller's African Greys listed at three different prices across three platforms">

          <h4>What Did We Actually Measure in August 2026?</h4>
          <p>Six sources, read on one afternoon, written down as we found them: two free-hosted sellers at the bottom, one seller across three platforms in the middle and upper range, and Google's own overview of this search at the top. We did not average them, because an average of a fiction and a fact is another fiction.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "how-we-measured-african-grey-listing-prices-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "how-we-measured-african-grey-listing-prices-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "how-we-measured-african-grey-listing-prices-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="How the same seller's listings were recorded across three platforms">

          <h5>The Same Aviary's Birds Carried a Four-Thousand-Dollar Spread in One Day</h5>
          <p>Four thousand dollars is not a discount or a promotion. It is a seller discovering what each platform's buyers will tolerate and pricing accordingly, which is legal, common, and worth knowing about before you take any single listing as the market rate for a parrot.</p>

          <h6>What We Measured: The Three Listings, Side by Side</h6>
          <p>Classified four and a half thousand. Own site seven and a half. Aggregator eight and a half. Same seller, same afternoon, same photographs of the same birds on all three.</p>

          <h3>What an Honest African Grey Price Is Made Of</h3>
          <p>Six things carry real cost, and they are the reason a floor exists at all: months of hand-feeding every few hours, the pre-sale veterinary examination, the screening panel, the sexing assay, the paper folder, and the crate a bird travels in. Strip any of them out and the price can fall. So can the bird's chances.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "what-an-african-grey-price-includes-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "what-an-african-grey-price-includes-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "what-an-african-grey-price-includes-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Six cost components behind an honest hand-raised African Grey price">

          <h4>Why Is an $850 African Grey a Warning Sign?</h4>
          <p>Because the feeding alone outruns it. A chick eats formula for months, somebody is awake for the night feeds, and the veterinary work happens whether or not a buyer appears. At eight hundred and fifty dollars the sums fail before the bird is weaned, which means one of three things is untrue: the bird, the testing, or the seller's existence.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "why-an-850-dollar-african-grey-is-a-warning-sign-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "why-an-850-dollar-african-grey-is-a-warning-sign-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "why-an-850-dollar-african-grey-is-a-warning-sign-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="What an $850 African Grey listing leaves out below the waterline">

          <h5>Hand-Feeding, Avian Vet Care, and Documentation Set the Real Floor</h5>
          <p>Our floor is {timnehRange.split("–")[0]} and it is arithmetic rather than positioning. Take the formula, the hours, the examination, the panel, the assay and the paperwork, and that is where a bird stops costing us money to place. A seller beneath it is either subsidising from somewhere or not doing the work.</p>

          <h6>What We Measured: Where the Low Listings Cluster</h6>
          <p>Both sub-thousand listings we found sat on free website hosts, showed no individual bird, named no veterinary practice and offered delivery free of charge. The pattern repeats because it costs nothing to publish.</p>

          <h3>How to Price-Check Any African Grey Breeder in Ten Minutes</h3>
          <p>Search the price, compare it against the range on this page, ask the seller to itemise, then ask to see the bird live. Four moves, ten minutes, and they settle most questions before any money moves. <a href="/trusted-african-grey-parrot-breeders/">Our own credentials page</a> is written so you can run those checks on us.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "price-check-any-african-grey-breeder-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "price-check-any-african-grey-breeder-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "price-check-any-african-grey-breeder-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Four moves that price-check an African Grey breeder in ten minutes">

          <h4>What Should You Ask Before You Send a Deposit?</h4>
          <p>Who owns the bird right now, can I see it live today, what paperwork comes with it, and how do you take payment. Four questions, and the last one carries the most weight, because a seller steering you toward a wire, a gift card or a peer-to-peer app has chosen the one route with no way back.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "what-to-ask-before-sending-a-deposit-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "what-to-ask-before-sending-a-deposit-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "what-to-ask-before-sending-a-deposit-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Four questions to ask a breeder before any deposit changes hands">
