        <!-- ===================== §3 · THE PAPERWORK ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="papers">
          <h2>What Paperwork Comes With an African Grey You Buy From Us?</h2>
          <p class="opener">Five documents and a banded leg, handed over together rather than posted on afterwards. Not one page-one competitor we checked publishes its documentation list, which is odd, because the folder is the part that proves a bird is what the listing says it is.</p>
          <img class="sec-img ph-wide" src={IMG + "african-grey-documents-folder-buy-page.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "african-grey-documents-folder-buy-page-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "african-grey-documents-folder-buy-page.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Sample DNA, health, CITES captive-bred and hatch records with personal fields redacted">
          <p class="cap">The documents shown are samples with personal fields redacted. Your bird's own paperwork is provided on request.</p>

          <h3>The Appendix I Certificate in Your Folder</h3>
          <p><StatementLabel kind="fact" source="https://cites.org/eng/app/appendices.php" />African Greys sit on Appendix I of the CITES treaty, which restricts international commercial trade in wild-caught birds. Ours are bred here, and the certificate in your folder records exactly that, which is what makes owning and transporting one inside this country straightforward.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "cites-appendix-one-certificate-in-your-folder-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "cites-appendix-one-certificate-in-your-folder-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "cites-appendix-one-certificate-in-your-folder-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="The CITES Appendix I certificate that travels in every buyer folder">

          <h4>Why Does CITES Matter When You Buy an African Grey?</h4>
          <p>Because it is the difference between a bird with a lawful history and a bird with none. The paperwork ties your parrot to a captive-bred origin, and a seller who cannot produce it is asking you to take the most checkable fact about your bird on trust. <a href="/cites-african-grey-documentation/">The documentation page</a> walks through each certificate in detail.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "why-cites-matters-when-you-buy-an-african-grey-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "why-cites-matters-when-you-buy-an-african-grey-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "why-cites-matters-when-you-buy-an-african-grey-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Why the treaty listing shapes a lawful captive-bred African Grey purchase">

          <h5>Appendix I Has Applied to Every African Grey Since January 2017</h5>
          <p>The uplisting took effect that month, so any bird offered as pre-treaty stock is either much older than it looks or being described dishonestly. It changed nothing about keeping a captive-bred parrot as a pet and a great deal about importing one.</p>

          <h6>On the Paperwork: What Appendix I Does and Does Not Mean</h6>
          <p>It does mean international commercial trade in wild-caught birds is barred. It does not mean you need a personal licence to keep a captive-bred bird at home, and it does not make your purchase a grey area. Keep the certificate with the rest of the folder.</p>

          <h3>Sex Testing and the Screens We Run</h3>
          <p><StatementLabel kind="observed" n={available.length} since={2014} />Every bird here is sexed by a laboratory before it is listed, and the same sample type carries the disease panel. You get the certificate with the laboratory's name on it rather than our opinion, which matters for a species that gives no reliable visual clue either way.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "dna-sexing-and-disease-screens-we-run-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "dna-sexing-and-disease-screens-we-run-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "dna-sexing-and-disease-screens-we-run-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="The three laboratory screens run on every bird before it is placed">

          <h4>How Do You Know the Sex of the Bird You Are Buying?</h4>
          <p>From the DNA result, never from the bird's appearance. Hens and cocks look alike at every age, so anyone telling you a bird is female because of its head shape or its tail is guessing. Ours arrive with the assay on paper, tied to the band number. <a href="/dna-tested-african-grey-for-sale/">The testing page</a> explains the method and what the fee covers.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "how-you-know-the-sex-of-your-african-grey-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "how-you-know-the-sex-of-your-african-grey-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "how-you-know-the-sex-of-your-african-grey-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Why a Grey's sex is confirmed by laboratory result and never by eye">

          <h3>Our USDA AWA Licence, and What It Lets You Verify</h3>
          <p>We hold a federal USDA licence under the Animal Welfare Act, and the number is yours on request so you can look us up rather than believe us. That is the point of a licence: it puts a third party between a seller's claim about themselves and a buyer's ability to check it.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "usda-awa-licence-you-can-verify-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "usda-awa-licence-you-can-verify-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "usda-awa-licence-you-can-verify-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="The USDA AWA licence a buyer can check before reserving a bird">

          <h3>Hatch Certificates and Band Numbers</h3>
          <p>The ring on your bird's leg went on as a chick and cannot be fitted to an adult, which is why it is the one identifier that cannot be moved between birds. Its number appears on the hatch record and on every certificate in the folder, so you can match paperwork to parrot yourself on the first evening.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "hatch-certificate-and-closed-band-number-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "hatch-certificate-and-closed-band-number-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "hatch-certificate-and-closed-band-number-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Closed band and hatch certificate, the two records that identify one bird">
        </section>

