        <!-- ===================== §5 · GETTING YOUR BIRD HOME ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="travel">
          <h2>Getting Your Bird From Our Aviary to Your City</h2>
          <p class="opener">Three routes, three prices, all printed. Shipping is how a bird reaches you rather than a reason to buy one, so we treat it as logistics to be explained plainly instead of a headline.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "three-ways-your-african-grey-travels-home-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "three-ways-your-african-grey-travels-home-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "three-ways-your-african-grey-travels-home-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Three routes an African Grey takes from the Midland aviary to a buyer">

          <p class="ship-line">Ships nationwide · <b>{airport}</b> airport cargo · <b>{home}</b> home delivery · <b>{cabin}</b> cabin with a flight nanny · collection here is free within a two to three hour drive.</p>

          <h3>Airport Cargo at {airport}</h3>
          <p>Most buyers choose this one. Your bird flies as manifested live-animal cargo in a crate meeting the airline's live-animal standard, on a direct flight wherever the route allows, and you collect at the cargo desk rather than at a baggage carousel. Delta, United and American all carry them.</p>
          <img class="sec-img ph-wide" src={IMG + "african-grey-iata-cargo-crate-airport.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "african-grey-iata-cargo-crate-airport-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "african-grey-iata-cargo-crate-airport.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="IATA-approved travel crate with an African Grey at the airline cargo counter">

          <h3>Home Delivery at {home}</h3>
          <p>The crate comes to the address you give us on a date we agree first, which suits buyers who would rather not drive to a cargo counter or who live a long way from one. It costs more because somebody makes the journey with your bird instead of the airline doing it.</p>
          <img class="sec-img ph-wide" src={IMG + "african-grey-home-delivery-courier-family.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "african-grey-home-delivery-courier-family-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "african-grey-home-delivery-courier-family.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Courier handing an African Grey carrier and document folder to a family at their door">

          <h3>Pickup in Midland, Texas</h3>
          <p>Free, and the gentlest option for the bird, which is why buyers within a few hours of us drive it regularly. You meet the parrot where it grew up, we go through the folder at the table, and nothing about the day involves a crate in a cargo hold. A cabin seat with a flight nanny is the third option at {cabin}, quoted per route because the nanny buys a ticket too.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "pickup-in-midland-texas-drive-time-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "pickup-in-midland-texas-drive-time-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "pickup-in-midland-texas-drive-time-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Collecting a bird in person from the Midland aviary gate">

          <h4>How Long Is an African Grey Actually in Transit?</h4>
          <p>On a direct routing, the flight itself is a few hours and the whole day usually ends with your bird home the same afternoon. We book around connections rather than through them, because that is where handling and temperature risk stack up.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "how-long-an-african-grey-is-in-transit-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "how-long-an-african-grey-is-in-transit-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "how-long-an-african-grey-is-in-transit-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Time a Grey spends in transit on a direct cargo routing">

          <h5>We Book Direct Flights and Keep Total Transit Under Eight Hours</h5>
          <p>Eight hours is our ceiling from crate to collection, and we will change the date rather than accept a routing that breaks it. If your nearest airport only takes connections, we will say so before you pay and look at the other two routes with you.</p>

          <h3>What Happens on Arrival Day</h3>
          <p>You collect, you bring your bird home, you put the crate in a quiet room and let it open in its own time. Food and water go in straight away, visitors do not. The first evening is for settling rather than for introductions, and the vet appointment you booked earlier does the rest.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "what-happens-on-arrival-day-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "what-happens-on-arrival-day-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "what-happens-on-arrival-day-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Four calm steps between landing and a bird's first quiet room">
        </section>

        <div class="ticket"><a class="ticket-cta" href="#reserve">Tell us where you are and we will quote the route <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>

