        <!-- ===================== §8 · FIVE CHECKS ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="checks">
          <h2>Five Checks That Catch a Fake Listing</h2>
          <p class="opener">A fraudulent listing fails the same five tests every time, and running them costs you ten minutes. We publish them knowing they apply to us as much as to anyone else selling a bird online.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "five-checks-fake-african-grey-listing-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "five-checks-fake-african-grey-listing-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "five-checks-fake-african-grey-listing-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Five verification checks that expose a fraudulent African Grey listing">

          <aside class="k4" aria-label="The five checks">
            <p class="k4-h">Run these before any money moves</p>
            <ul class="k4-list">
              <li><span set:html={tick} /> A street address you can look up, not just a city</li>
              <li><span set:html={tick} /> A licence number you can verify with the issuer</li>
              <li><span set:html={tick} /> A live video call with the specific bird, today</li>
              <li><span set:html={tick} /> The documents shown before payment, not promised after</li>
              <li><span set:html={tick} /> A payment route that leaves you recourse</li>
            </ul>
          </aside>

          <h3>What to Ask, and What to Refuse</h3>
          <p>Ask for the address, the licence, the live call and the folder. Refuse a wire transfer, a gift card, a peer-to-peer app and any story about why the bird cannot be seen moving today. The refusals matter more than the questions, because a seller only needs one irreversible payment route to disappear down.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "what-to-ask-and-what-to-refuse-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "what-to-ask-and-what-to-refuse-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "what-to-ask-and-what-to-refuse-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="What to ask a seller and what to refuse outright when buying">

          <h4>Can a Video Call Prove a Bird Is Real?</h4>
          <p>It is the single hardest thing for a fake listing to produce. Ask for the call now rather than later, ask the seller to move the bird onto a hand, and ask a question they have to answer live. A recorded clip proves a parrot existed somewhere once; a live call proves this one exists today.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "can-a-video-call-prove-the-bird-is-real-infographic.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "can-a-video-call-prove-the-bird-is-real-infographic-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "can-a-video-call-prove-the-bird-is-real-infographic.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Proving a named bird exists through a live video call">

          <h5>Only a Verifiable Address Has Ever Stopped One of These</h5>
          <p>Every other signal can be faked cheaply: photographs, testimonials, a professional-looking site, even a licence number copied from a real aviary. A physical place with people at it is the one thing a fraudulent seller cannot manufacture, which is why <a href="/how-to-avoid-african-grey-parrot-scams/">our guide to spotting a scam</a> starts there rather than with the price.</p>
        </section>

